{"video_id": "tqsG3YN8OEY", "source_file": "If You Don_t Understand Bonds, You Don_t Understand Money [tqsG3YN8OEY].mp4", "source_sha256": "c731dfa51ec8ba6c0e1b0c9b2f7011c12a7f4750ea8450677a4e831d1348d3d6", "local_dur": 57.4, "model": "base", "engine": "openai-whisper", "status": "ASR_UNCERTIFIED", "device": "cpu", "elapsed_s": 4.7, "language": "en", "text": " But right now, while everyone's distracted watching stocks go vertical and the AI hype, the adult in the room quietly just pulled the plug on the party. Because the movement in the yields is the real story. What matters is the underlying mechanics of why they moved. The number you see in the headlines is just the symptom. And in its simplest form, a bond is just an IOU. It can be the government, a company, or anyone who wants to borrow money. They issue a piece of paper that promises to pay back a fixed amount at a fixed maturity date. People bid for that paper, and the price those buyers are willing to pay determines the yield. So the relationship between a bond's price and its yield are inversely related. If buyers are scrambling to buy the bond, the price gets bid up and the yield falls. If buyers aren't interested, the price has to drop to attract new buyers and the yield rises. So when yields are rising across the board, it means buyers are demanding a higher return before they'll lock their money up. And when rates rise this aggressively, there's consequences.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 4.52, "text": " But right now, while everyone's distracted watching stocks go vertical and the AI hype,", "tokens": [50364, 583, 558, 586, 11, 1339, 1518, 311, 21658, 1976, 12966, 352, 9429, 293, 264, 7318, 24144, 11, 50603], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12340729376849006, "compression_ratio": 1.654434250764526, "no_speech_prob": 0.007378564681857824, "words": [{"word": " But", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.12, "probability": 0.7040521502494812}, {"word": " right", "start": 0.12, "end": 0.2